# {{ title }}

YAMD uses BibTeX for citations. Point a note at a `.bib` file in the front matter, cite with `[@key]`, and the extension formats the references list for you in any of five styles.

::toc{depth=2}

---

## Setting up a bibliography

Add two keys to the note's front matter:

```yamd
---
title: My essay
bibliography: refs.bib
citation-style: apa
---
```

`bibliography:` is a path to a `.bib` file, relative to the note *or* the
workspace root. `citation-style:` is one of:

| Value | Style |
| ----- | ----- |
| `apa` (default) | Author (Year) |
| `mla` | Author page number |
| `chicago` | Superscript footnote number |
| `ieee` | `[N]` numbered reference |
| `vancouver` | Superscript numbered |

---

## Citing a source

Use `[@key]` anywhere in the text. The key is the BibTeX entry's identifier.

This note's `refs.bib` contains an entry with key `cormen2022`. Here is a
citation: [@cormen2022].

With a locator (page, chapter, section…):

```yamd
[@cormen2022, p. 342]
[@cormen2022, ch. 4]
[@cormen2022, pp. 10–15]
```

Supported locators: `p.`, `pp.`, `ch.`, `sec.`, `fig.`, `eq.`, `no.`, `vol.`,
`ed.`, `trans.`

---

## The reference list

Put `::bibliography` where you want the list to appear. If you leave it out,
the list is appended at the end of the document automatically.

```yamd
## References

::bibliography
```

---

## Cross-referencing figures, tables and equations

`[@prefix:id]` creates a numbered cross-reference. The prefix tells YAMD what
type of thing is being referenced:

| Prefix | Refers to |
| ------ | --------- |
| `fig:` | `:::figure {#fig:x}` |
| `tbl:` | `:::table {#tbl:x}` |
| `eq:` | A numbered equation block |
| `sec:` | A heading with `{#sec:x}` |
| `def:` | `:::definition {#def:x}` |
| `thm:` | `:::theorem {#thm:x}` |
| `lem:` | `:::lemma {#lem:x}` |
| `ex:` | `:::example {#ex:x}` |
| `q:` | `:::question {#q:x}` |
| `rem:` | `:::remark {#rem:x}` |

**Example:**

```yamd
:::figure {caption="The sorting network" #fig:sort}
![Sorting network](sort.png)
:::

See [@fig:sort] for the diagram.
```

---

## Adding a citation from the internet

Run **YAMD: Add Citation** from the command palette. Paste a DOI, arXiv ID or ISBN:

| You enter | Where it looks |
| --------- | -------------- |
| `10.1145/3453483.3454023` | doi.org |
| `2310.00024` | arxiv.org |
| `9780262046305` | openlibrary.org |

The extension fetches the BibTeX entry and appends it to the note's `.bib` file (creating `refs.bib` in the same folder if the note has no `bibliography:` key).

If you're offline, the command falls back to a text box where you can paste a
BibTeX entry manually.

---

